    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. Another thing we're seeing are cases where the skin shows quite a bit of discoloration—lots of patchy, dark spots and almost a greenish-grey undertone in certain areas. It's really hard to map those to any of the standard inflammatory or pigmentary disorder categories. If the appearance is visually very distinct or \"otherworldly\" in its irregularity, how much weight should the coder give to the visual pattern versus the documented clinical diagnosis to stay compliant?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In cases of visually distinct but diagnostically unclear lesions, the document integrity of the clinical diagnosis generally carries more weight than the visual pattern alone; however, a good-faith effort to describe the specific color and texture (like the greenish-grey undertone) is required to justify any deviation from a standard code. If the documentation explicitly states a diagnosis and provides sufficient descriptive detail to map it to a code cluster (even if atypical), you should use that specific code rather than defaulting to an unspecified \"other\" category. Always verify with your medical director or coder's guideline committee if the atypical presentation defies standard coding rules, as misclassification due to unresolved visual confusion can lead to audit findings.",
      "source": "target",
